---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../../../styles/equipment.css';
const base='/equipment/14-05/', spec='https://www.optics11life.com/wp-content/uploads/Optics11-Life-Piuma-Brochure-BRPIU202401v1.pdf';
---
<Layout pageTitle="Piuma · spherical nanoindentation" description="Map two inert elastic references with a virtual Optics11 Piuma and compare apparent reduced modulus with independently known values." wide={true}>
<article class="equipment pi">
<nav class="eq-nav"><a href="/simulations/equipment/14-05/">← Equipment 14-05</a><a href="/simulations/equipment/">Equipment register</a><a href="#pi-ref">References and verification</a></nav>
<header class="eq-mast"><span class="eq-meta">14-05 · ELASTIC METROLOGY / FIRST PASS</span><h2>Optics11 Life Piuma</h2><p>Standalone tabletop Piuma with a spherical fiber-optic cantilever and two inert teaching coupons. Approach the surface and compare their fitted reduced moduli; no biological specimen or hardware calibration is involved.</p></header>
<div class="panel"><section><h3>Instrument</h3><div id="pi-view" aria-label="Orbitable metric model of stand, optical head, cantilever and xy stage"></div><div class="bar"><button data-pi-camera="overall">Overall</button><button data-pi-camera="probe">Probe and stage</button><button data-pi-camera="rear">Rear</button><button id="pi-vr" hidden>Enter VR</button></div><p id="pi-ready" role="status">Loading metric GLB…</p><p>Drag to orbit, or click the cyan teaching button to approach/retract. The visual stroke is exaggerated; the solver operates in metric micrometer-scale coordinates. Rear fittings are inferred.</p></section>
<section><h3>Elastic calibration exercise</h3><div class="fields">
<label>Site<select id="pi-site"><option value="0">A · (−2, +0.7) mm</option><option value="1">B · (+2, −0.7) mm</option></select></label>
<label>Assumed tip radius (µm)<input id="pi-radius" value="25" step="any" type="number" min="3" max="250"/></label>
<label>Assumed spring stiffness (N/m)<input id="pi-k" value=".5" step="any" type="number" min=".02" max="200"/></label>
<label>Peak indentation (µm)<input id="pi-depth" value="1.5" step="any" type="number" min=".01" max="90"/></label>
<label>Assumed thickness (mm)<input id="pi-thick" value="2" step="any" type="number" min=".001" max="100"/></label></div>
<div class="bar"><button id="pi-load">Unload fixture</button><button id="pi-move">Approach</button><button id="pi-run">Acquire current site</button><button id="pi-map" class="primary">Run two-site reference</button></div><p id="pi-status" aria-live="polite">Retracted. Run the two-site reference or approach a selected site.</p></section></div>
<section><h3>Force–indentation and spatial results</h3><div class="panel"><canvas width="680" height="300" id="pi-chart" aria-label="Measured model force and fitted traces versus indentation"></canvas><div><output id="pi-results">No map acquired.</output><div class="bar"><button id="pi-json" disabled>Export map (JSON)</button><button id="pi-csv" disabled>Export curves (CSV)</button></div></div></div></section>
<section id="pi-ref"><h3>Model and evidence</h3>
<p>The solver uses <strong>F = k·bend</strong> and <strong>δ = travel − bend − contact zero</strong>. It fits the slope of <code>F = (4/3) E* √R δ^(3/2)</code> at a known contact zero. In the example the synthetic probe’s radius and spring are 25 µm and 0.5 N/m. Editing either assumed calibration changes the fit without altering the original virtual readings. E* is the reduced contact modulus of this model, not a general material Young’s modulus. No adhesion, viscoelasticity, finite-layer correction or uncertainty calibration is included; the fixture rejects δ/R &gt; 0.1 or thickness below ten contact radii.</p>
<p><a href={spec+'#page=1'}>Overall photograph</a> · <a href={spec+'#page=4'}>Optical fiber and cantilever close-up</a> · <a href={spec+'#page=12'}>Vendor specification table</a> · <a href="https://emtoolbox.nist.gov/Publications/NationalStandardsLaboratoryTechnicalPaperNo25.asp">NIST contact mechanics reference</a></p>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Indenter nominal W × D × H</td><td>120 × 150 × 280 mm</td><td>Manufacturer exterior anchor; unlisted part dimensions inferred.</td></tr><tr><td>XY / probe options</td><td>12 × 12 mm stage, 3–250 µm spherical radius, 0.02–200 N/m</td><td>Hardware ranges are not a claim of model validity outside the exercise.</td></tr><tr><td>Independent numeric trace</td><td>Expected 12,000 Pa; fitted 12,000 Pa</td><td>≤0.0001 Pa analytical tolerance, not instrument precision.</td></tr><tr><td>Two-site map</td><td>A 12 kPa, B 48 kPa; both coordinates retained</td><td>Model check; not bench-measured.</td></tr><tr><td>Thin-layer boundary</td><td>Reject</td><td>A Hertz half-space is not appropriate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><a href={base+'verification.json'}>Verification</a> · <a href={base+'reference-cases.json'}>Locked reference values</a> · <a href={base+'example-map.json'}>Example map and trace format</a> · <a href={base+'piuma.glb'} download>Metric GLB</a> · <a href={base+'model.py'} download>Editable model</a> · <a href={base+'xr-manifest.json'}>VR action contract</a></p><p>Optional VR uses the same approach action. No physical headset or hardware was tested. No downstream reader is required for this first-pass exercise.</p>
</section></article></Layout>
